WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:05.540
- Welcome everyone to Brilliant
Minds, Bold Questions,

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:08.650
a five-part virtual event
series exploring questions

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:11.440
being addressed by some of
the world's top researchers.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.510
Each talk pairs speakers and moderators

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:15.890
from across discipline
sectors in the world

00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:18.930
to explore the question,
shaping our future.

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:20.210
If you're not familiar with us,

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:22.600
CIFAR is a Canadian
based global organization

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:25.380
that convenes extraordinary
minds to address the most

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:28.370
important questions facing
science and humanity.

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:29.660
My name is Rachel Parker

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:32.550
and I'm Senior Director
of Research at CIFAR.

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:35.260
And it's my pleasure to
introduce to you Dan Breznitz

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:37.010
and Woody Powell today.

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:39.010
Dan is an expert on the globalization

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:40.890
of rapid innovation based industries

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:42.470
and has led pioneering research

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:45.760
on the distributional impact
of innovation policies.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:48.460
Woody's interests focus on the processes

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:50.810
through which ideas and
practices are transferred

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:53.330
across organizations
and the role of networks

00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:56.240
in facilitating or hindering innovation.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.840
Today, we'll explore growth
and equity, can we have both?

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:02.650
Woody, I'd like to turn it over to you now

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:03.950
to ask the first question.

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:06.570
- Sure, thank you, Rachel.

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:08.870
Dan, thank you for your new book

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:10.860
on "Innovation in Real Places".

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:11.820
We'll talk a little bit

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:14.500
about what you think of is unreal places.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:18.010
I have a feeling my territory
out here in California

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:20.250
is one such place in your mind.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:23.540
But you looked at innovation
that's not focused on

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:26.440
Silicon Valley, or Seattle, or Schengen

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:29.700
but rather on opportunities
and communities

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:33.740
that don't normally get
considered when one thinks

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:36.530
about them as centres of prosperity.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:39.050
Because I want to start by
asking you isn't innovation

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:42.920
fundamentally about breaking
rules and challenging

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.180
existing ways of doing things?

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:48.793
And does it always have to
have an outsider quality to it?

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:51.330
- Thank you, Woody.

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:55.393
And yes, just by your
question it is very clear

00:01:55.393 --> 00:01:58.963
that you come from Silicon
Valley, which is slightly unreal.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:04.840
No, so I would say that
unfortunately in the last few years

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:07.810
our debate, especially publicly
debate about innovation,

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:10.550
has been completely divorced from reality

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:12.970
and we forgot what innovation is

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:16.400
where is that thing
which is called invention

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:20.000
coming up with new ideas
for product or services.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.410
Innovation is actually putting

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:26.940
that ideas into reality
in the marketplace.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:30.640
And that includes all
stages from coming up

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:35.200
with an idea to after
service, production, assembly,

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:38.680
design versus the real innovation.

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:42.500
And if you want to see a
real innovation in action

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:47.260
creating widespread prosperity,
forget Silicon Valley.

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:49.520
Come with me to Taiwan.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:51.130
Come with me to Finland.

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:52.960
Come with me to Germany.

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:55.930
Indeed, you know, unless
you really like Tesla.

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:57.940
And by the way, even if you like Tesla,

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:00.140
open it up and you'll
find out that most of it

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:03.310
is Bosch, a German company.

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:05.973
I know Allan doesn't like
that but that's the truth.

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:10.450
And that's where you
really have innovation.

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:14.752
And that through that
movement, through those stages,

00:03:14.752 --> 00:03:19.752
through that constant and
continuous improvement, changing,

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:24.400
tweaking, is where
you've got real advantage

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:28.630
to welfare and real economic
growth and prosperity.

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:31.240
Just think for a moment about the millions

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:35.880
of engineering hours that
have been put into innovation

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:40.880
of how to improve the CPU,
the memory, the fibre optics,

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:45.520
the amount of data you
can move into fibre optics

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:47.773
so you and I can now talk on Zoom.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:52.730
- You said a number of things
here I want to explore.

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:55.870
You expanded the idea of innovation

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:58.880
to think about invention and
every stage of production.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:02.630
You also added this idea
of continuous improvement.

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:05.240
Those are two things that
are sort of different

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:08.523
than, you know, the one
big disruptive idea.

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:13.770
You also mentioned countries
like Taiwan, Finland,

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:18.320
Israel is another example
that are very diverse

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:22.700
on some dimensions but seem
to be places quite different

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:27.700
from say the traditional
centres and the tech capitals

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:31.350
where the gains of innovation
are shared more widely.

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:32.840
So what's the relationship

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:36.280
between this continuous
improvement at every stage

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:38.640
of production and being in a society

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:43.030
where more people benefit
from these advancements?

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:47.923
- Sure, so let me go back to
the places that you mentioned.

00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:52.350
I think that one thing
that we have to understand

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:55.710
and that's why in the book
there is another title

00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:57.803
which is about globalization.

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:01.010
Once you have globalization

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:03.610
and the way we have a globalization

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:07.210
meaning that you take
a product or service,

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:12.210
you basically now defragment
it to several stages

00:05:15.681 --> 00:05:20.280
in each country or place in the
world now does those things.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:21.130
So let's think about

00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:23.960
the semiconductor industry for a moment.

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:26.840
The US has a beautiful one.

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:28.320
Israel has a great one.

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:29.930
Taiwan has a great one.

00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:32.010
Korea has a great one.

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:35.800
And yet in those places
some of the same companies

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:38.970
working all those places but what they do

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:41.800
in those places are completely different.

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:43.890
So in Silicon Valley and in Israel,

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:47.190
they try to put new ideas on silicon.

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:50.000
But they, as we now know thanks to Intel

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:51.603
and what happened in the United States,

00:05:51.603 --> 00:05:54.690
without the Taiwanese company called TSMC,

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:57.750
we no longer know how to
put those ideas on silicon.

00:05:57.750 --> 00:05:59.830
And that's where Taiwan excels.

00:05:59.830 --> 00:06:04.830
In Korea, when you buy any
mobile phone, including an Apple,

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:09.560
the second highest amount of
profit now occurs to Samsung

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:12.080
which control critical things

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:15.033
that go to every smartphone on earth.

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:19.870
That also tells you where you have, say,

00:06:19.870 --> 00:06:22.150
power over the network.

00:06:22.150 --> 00:06:25.290
And let me just in this small examples,

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:28.810
Taiwan and Korea might
have actually more power

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:31.140
on the whole network on the mobile phones

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:33.270
than Silicon Valley right now.

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:37.400
The second is those different
kinds of innovation,

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:40.650
let us say different
skills, different finance,

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:43.560
provide to the community
completely different

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:45.130
kinds of employment.

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:48.070
So you mentioned Israel,
which is a small country

00:06:48.070 --> 00:06:51.080
and everybody thinks is
a great economic miracle,

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:53.880
versus Taiwan, let's say, or Finland.

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:55.760
Unfortunately, as somebody who has done

00:06:55.760 --> 00:07:00.020
a lot of research on all
but also grew up in Israel,

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:02.490
what happened in Israel because Israel

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:06.460
imitate Silicon Valley, might
be the only case of a place

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:09.920
which actually managed to
imitate Silicon Valley fully.

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:13.600
Israel is now one of the most,
just like Silicon Valley,

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:16.420
one of most unequal places on earth.

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:18.090
As a matter of fact, in the same year

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:20.870
as you have this massive economic miracle,

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:22.963
the Israeli society went that way.

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:29.280
And right now, one out of
every five household in Israel

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:31.270
is under the poverty line

00:07:31.270 --> 00:07:33.980
and basically on a treadmill to nowhere.

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:38.050
While only the graduates
of the best universities,

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:40.990
best technological units, the financier,

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:43.450
and celebrity chefs, are enjoying

00:07:43.450 --> 00:07:46.550
high-end Silicon Valley wages

00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:49.230
and the lottery ticket called, you know,

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:50.643
stock options for an IPO.

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:55.360
So I think one of the real
questions that real places

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:59.160
should have is why do you
want to have innovation?

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:03.120
Why spend taxpayers' money on innovation

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:04.583
if you're a small city?

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:08.570
And if the reason is I
want to create prosperity

00:08:08.570 --> 00:08:13.570
for my citizen, my residents,
imitating Silicon Valley,

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:16.993
even if you can be successful,
might be wrong answer.

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:23.620
- We saw a good example of
what we were describing with.

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:26.220
Semiconductors with US auto makers

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:29.460
where they're having to slow
or stop production for trucks

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:34.040
because of the lack of access to chips

00:08:34.040 --> 00:08:35.350
from Taiwan at the moment.

00:08:35.350 --> 00:08:40.250
So these global production
networks are hugely important.

00:08:40.250 --> 00:08:45.010
Okay, so Israel is a case of
replication without the gains.

00:08:45.010 --> 00:08:48.830
Let's think more about how
innovation could be pursued

00:08:48.830 --> 00:08:51.540
and the gains can be more widely shared.

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:54.380
So one of the messages
I took from your book

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:59.380
was don't try to emulate these,
you know, canonical places.

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:01.700
But then I'm sure people want to know

00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:04.050
what dreams should Cleveland, Ohio have?

00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:06.635
Brampton, Ontario or Birmingham, UK,

00:09:06.635 --> 00:09:11.635
what can be the ways they
pursue innovation policies

00:09:13.430 --> 00:09:16.520
in the context of old industries?

00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:17.997
Let us say.

00:09:17.997 --> 00:09:20.827
- So first, let's talk
about an old industry.

00:09:20.827 --> 00:09:22.600
And let me give an example of one

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:24.743
of the oldest industries in the world.

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:27.340
Shoes.

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:29.856
You know, the things you put on your foot.

00:09:29.856 --> 00:09:30.730
(Woody laughs)

00:09:30.730 --> 00:09:35.113
And let's talk about
high-end luxury woman shoes.

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:39.090
And once you look at that industry,

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:42.280
which by the way create
a lot of prosperity,

00:09:42.280 --> 00:09:44.160
you found out that one of the places

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:47.840
which has the highest and
the most sustained prosperity

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:50.680
for people who not just
designer from, you know,

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:55.259
the top-end schools,
is Riviera del Brenta,

00:09:55.259 --> 00:09:57.260
in Italy, just about Venice.

00:09:57.260 --> 00:10:00.150
And the reason is that they focus

00:10:00.150 --> 00:10:03.140
on what I call the second
stage of innovation.

00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:06.190
So if you are a wonderful
designer from Milan,

00:10:06.190 --> 00:10:10.920
or Paris, or New York, and
you design a really cool shoe

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:14.357
and you say, "Okay, now
this shoe has to be made.

00:10:14.357 --> 00:10:17.977
"I have to be able to sell
it for an X amount of price.

00:10:17.977 --> 00:10:21.267
"And a real human being
has to wear that shoes.

00:10:21.267 --> 00:10:24.497
"And if that really human
being is paying 2000 euros,

00:10:24.497 --> 00:10:27.267
"they might actually want to
walk on it from time to time.

00:10:27.267 --> 00:10:29.307
"So it can not just explode."

00:10:30.950 --> 00:10:34.940
The place that will make
that happen for you is Brenta

00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:38.980
and its set of suppliers,
very high-end suppliers,

00:10:38.980 --> 00:10:40.620
very high-end.

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:44.530
If you want to think about
it designing of how you make

00:10:44.530 --> 00:10:49.120
an idea into a product,
running up into production,

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:51.303
very high-end production,
selling it to you.

00:10:51.303 --> 00:10:53.940
And by the way, Woody, if
you're not creative enough

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:56.690
this year and you had
only a hundred new shoes

00:10:56.690 --> 00:11:00.480
and you need 200, you can
also get the Brenta people

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:02.570
to come up with another hundred.

00:11:02.570 --> 00:11:06.330
And that's very wide prosperity
for people from those

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:09.120
who can actually design
shoes, the creative,

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:11.160
to the people who know
how to put, you know,

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:14.083
the sole to the end of a shoe and sell it.

00:11:15.410 --> 00:11:18.393
- Sounds like a story of
the rediscovery of craft.

00:11:22.794 --> 00:11:24.760
And then one way, you
know, that rediscovery

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:27.690
is a form of invention taking
something that's been around

00:11:27.690 --> 00:11:31.490
for a long time, applying
it to new settings,

00:11:31.490 --> 00:11:34.250
paying attention to skill
so that more and more people

00:11:34.250 --> 00:11:37.250
are involved in learning that skill.

00:11:37.250 --> 00:11:41.160
Is there a difference in
our educational systems

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:46.010
between places that
focus more on, you know,

00:11:46.010 --> 00:11:48.480
high-end computer science and finance

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:52.717
versus those that are
more skilled (indistinct)?

00:11:55.150 --> 00:11:57.300
- To a agree, yes.

00:11:57.300 --> 00:11:59.692
And definitely in the case of Brenta.

00:11:59.692 --> 00:12:03.710
But if I go to Taiwan,
since we mentioned it again,

00:12:03.710 --> 00:12:05.920
Taiwan is also known for,

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:10.000
I don't know if you ride
bikes, but if you ride bikes

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:10.933
especially--
- I'll buy one.

00:12:10.933 --> 00:12:11.766
(Woody laughs)

00:12:11.766 --> 00:12:14.930
- Especially if you move to
mountain bikes a few years ago,

00:12:14.930 --> 00:12:17.600
by definition, you had a Taiwanese product

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:20.963
mostly almost surely from Giant.

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:24.430
And what you see in Taiwan is, again,

00:12:24.430 --> 00:12:27.720
they took a very old product

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:31.573
basically it's a diamond frame
designed 200, 300 years ago,

00:12:32.670 --> 00:12:34.550
and infuse it with new technologies,

00:12:34.550 --> 00:12:36.480
in that case, new materials.

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:41.300
So you can make the bike go on off-road.

00:12:41.300 --> 00:12:44.817
It was light enough for people
who are not looking like

00:12:44.817 --> 00:12:47.140
you know, Superman to actually carry it

00:12:47.140 --> 00:12:49.340
into the mountain and
start mountain biking.

00:12:50.980 --> 00:12:54.250
What is interesting here and
the reason I wanted to say,

00:12:54.250 --> 00:12:57.770
you need extremely
high-end material science

00:12:57.770 --> 00:12:59.170
in order to do that.

00:12:59.170 --> 00:13:03.173
But you also need the people who produces.

00:13:05.450 --> 00:13:10.450
That means that while you have
a very high-end universities

00:13:10.985 --> 00:13:15.985
and almost fundamental research
and researchers in Taiwan,

00:13:16.360 --> 00:13:19.340
what they do in Taiwan,
how they use those skills

00:13:19.340 --> 00:13:21.340
and the incentives that they get

00:13:21.340 --> 00:13:22.920
in order to use those skills,

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:25.490
finance and other are different.

00:13:25.490 --> 00:13:27.690
So again, depending on your stage

00:13:27.690 --> 00:13:29.450
and depending how you attack it

00:13:29.450 --> 00:13:32.950
and depending if you're
Cleveland and you have

00:13:32.950 --> 00:13:36.410
an amazing university and
an amazing health system,

00:13:36.410 --> 00:13:39.190
or if you're Hamilton,
Ontario, and you have the same,

00:13:39.190 --> 00:13:42.740
or you're in a place
which closer to Brenta,

00:13:42.740 --> 00:13:43.840
if you want to think about,

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:47.660
and you still have those craft
skills, as you call them,

00:13:47.660 --> 00:13:49.770
design a strategy around that.

00:13:49.770 --> 00:13:53.270
Design a strategy around a
different stage of production.

00:13:53.270 --> 00:13:56.130
Do not assume that if you
don't become New York,

00:13:56.130 --> 00:13:58.190
Silicon Valley, or Tel Aviv,

00:13:58.190 --> 00:14:00.333
you cannot innovate and gain prosperity.

00:14:01.290 --> 00:14:04.970
And unfortunately, as
we have talked before,

00:14:04.970 --> 00:14:08.610
that conversation no longer
exist with policymakers.

00:14:08.610 --> 00:14:10.010
They all think that if they are not

00:14:10.010 --> 00:14:12.150
Silicon Valley of the North,

00:14:12.150 --> 00:14:13.370
you know, I'm in Canada

00:14:13.370 --> 00:14:17.200
so at least 15 cities claim that title,

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:18.720
we are not innovative.

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:20.230
And I think it's a misservice

00:14:21.070 --> 00:14:24.400
to our own residents to think that way.

00:14:24.400 --> 00:14:25.960
- It's a really important insight.

00:14:25.960 --> 00:14:27.773
I take from it a couple of things.

00:14:29.010 --> 00:14:32.630
One, through all the stories
in your wonderful book,

00:14:32.630 --> 00:14:35.440
there is this focus on continuous updating

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:38.000
whether it's bicycles or shoes.

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:39.390
You know, so there's a drive

00:14:39.390 --> 00:14:42.780
to constantly make these things better

00:14:42.780 --> 00:14:47.610
And better doesn't involve disruption,

00:14:47.610 --> 00:14:50.390
often involves making things better

00:14:50.390 --> 00:14:53.490
so the consumers will,
you know, like them more,

00:14:53.490 --> 00:14:55.690
they'll be lighter,
they'll be more durable.

00:14:55.690 --> 00:15:00.490
There are a lot of positives
for many people in that.

00:15:00.490 --> 00:15:02.450
You use the word incentives.

00:15:02.450 --> 00:15:04.880
Okay, we typically think of incentives

00:15:04.880 --> 00:15:09.430
as tax breaks, government funding.

00:15:09.430 --> 00:15:10.700
I don't think those are the kinds

00:15:10.700 --> 00:15:12.313
of incentives you mean though?

00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:18.480
- I will say, let's be very
clear, some of them are.

00:15:18.860 --> 00:15:21.103
Okay, there's a lot of
role for government.

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:23.380
And much of a role for government

00:15:23.380 --> 00:15:26.870
especially with innovation which is risk,

00:15:26.870 --> 00:15:30.980
is indeed with creating those incentives

00:15:30.980 --> 00:15:32.960
that you just talked about.

00:15:32.960 --> 00:15:35.140
But the other set of
incentives, and again,

00:15:35.140 --> 00:15:37.060
it's a public private policy,

00:15:37.060 --> 00:15:42.060
is a vision of how you want your society,

00:15:43.100 --> 00:15:48.100
a vision of status and what
those people want to have.

00:15:49.990 --> 00:15:54.990
But also a set of regulations
that allow or disallow

00:15:55.460 --> 00:15:57.390
different kinds of business models

00:15:57.390 --> 00:16:00.973
even if a lower short term profit.

00:16:02.810 --> 00:16:05.950
Which I think is really
important right now

00:16:05.950 --> 00:16:08.483
speaking about
semiconductors manufacturing.

00:16:10.420 --> 00:16:14.510
Intel, it may be one last American company

00:16:14.510 --> 00:16:18.870
that can announce whether
it is investing $20 billion

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:21.870
in having fabrication
or manufacturing sites

00:16:21.870 --> 00:16:25.960
for semiconductors without
the CEO being fired

00:16:27.090 --> 00:16:30.300
within five minutes by
Wall Street analysts

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:31.200
and their friends.

00:16:33.280 --> 00:16:34.950
And those are the kinds of incentives

00:16:34.950 --> 00:16:38.840
which we don't talk about
in a polite company.

00:16:38.840 --> 00:16:41.993
But I think with, especially
in North America, we should.

00:16:44.800 --> 00:16:48.560
- I hear you doing two
things with that statement.

00:16:48.560 --> 00:16:52.390
One, telling us important lesson

00:16:52.390 --> 00:16:57.170
which is that we should care
as much about the person

00:16:57.170 --> 00:17:01.400
who builds the bike, the
person who crafts the shoes,

00:17:01.400 --> 00:17:04.160
the person who bravely
makes a decision to invest

00:17:04.160 --> 00:17:09.160
in a new fab plant, as
we should, you know,

00:17:09.230 --> 00:17:12.490
the 26 year old, Vinder Ken,
who comes up with, you know,

00:17:12.490 --> 00:17:17.490
the new way to take a vacation
or to have food delivery.

00:17:17.740 --> 00:17:22.090
So that's a rethinking of who
the stars of innovation are.

00:17:22.090 --> 00:17:26.260
There's also lots of discussion
though about innovation

00:17:27.890 --> 00:17:32.890
as something that leads
to gains for only a few.

00:17:34.500 --> 00:17:37.470
And I think that's, well I
will turn to the audience now

00:17:37.470 --> 00:17:39.323
and Rachel you're in charge of that,

00:17:40.230 --> 00:17:41.730
to help us think more broadly

00:17:41.730 --> 00:17:46.020
about what are ways to make
innovation more inclusive.

00:17:46.020 --> 00:17:47.110
- Before we turn to the audience,

00:17:47.110 --> 00:17:49.873
and I have a question
that I wanted to ask for.

00:17:51.595 --> 00:17:53.653
Do you want to respond to Dan or.

00:17:55.070 --> 00:17:57.840
- Sure, so let me just be very clear

00:17:57.840 --> 00:18:00.720
of that's exactly the reason why we,

00:18:00.720 --> 00:18:03.980
and when I say we I mean you, I, Rachel,

00:18:03.980 --> 00:18:08.980
and another 12 people, have
created a new CIFAR programme

00:18:09.070 --> 00:18:11.490
which is called Innovation, Equity,

00:18:11.490 --> 00:18:13.590
and the Future of Prosperity.

00:18:13.590 --> 00:18:18.010
And it's exactly that fear that we have

00:18:18.010 --> 00:18:21.360
or that many have that innovation,

00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:24.670
at least in last 20
years, means that the few

00:18:24.670 --> 00:18:27.257
and as Woody has very politely said,

00:18:27.257 --> 00:18:29.450
the few who are 26 year old,

00:18:29.450 --> 00:18:32.710
and the graduate of Stanford,
Berkeley, and the UFT,

00:18:32.710 --> 00:18:35.730
so not exactly the people
we should worry about,

00:18:35.730 --> 00:18:39.173
have all the gains and the rest
of the population does not.

00:18:40.110 --> 00:18:43.010
And what we're doing in the network

00:18:43.010 --> 00:18:44.440
and what we are thinking about

00:18:44.440 --> 00:18:48.670
is actually figuring out
whether it's true or not.

00:18:48.670 --> 00:18:51.530
And I think it's clearly not.

00:18:51.530 --> 00:18:55.880
And then wondering how what
would be called the incentives

00:18:55.880 --> 00:18:58.010
or what other people might call it

00:18:58.010 --> 00:19:00.300
different innovation policies,

00:19:00.300 --> 00:19:04.290
lead different places, countries

00:19:04.290 --> 00:19:07.540
down at different trajectories
of how they innovate

00:19:07.540 --> 00:19:10.970
and how the gains of
innovation are distributed

00:19:10.970 --> 00:19:13.023
for other societies and for whom.

00:19:14.010 --> 00:19:17.530
And that's exactly the
discussion we're starting

00:19:17.530 --> 00:19:20.423
at CIFAR across multiple disciplines.

00:19:22.890 --> 00:19:24.433
- So the question that I have for you both

00:19:24.433 --> 00:19:28.410
is in some ways a followup
to that and kind of thinking

00:19:28.410 --> 00:19:31.250
through different types of innovation

00:19:32.190 --> 00:19:35.143
although potentially
with a different meaning.

00:19:36.470 --> 00:19:39.350
So everyone seems to be putting
their favourite adjective

00:19:39.350 --> 00:19:40.710
in front of the word innovation

00:19:40.710 --> 00:19:41.930
whether it's social innovation,

00:19:41.930 --> 00:19:44.590
frugal innovation, inclusive innovation.

00:19:44.590 --> 00:19:46.910
And so I'm curious to hear your thoughts.

00:19:46.910 --> 00:19:50.150
First, are these important
or necessary distinctions

00:19:50.150 --> 00:19:51.773
to make and if they are, why?

00:19:53.980 --> 00:19:56.590
- I'll start and Dan can pick up.

00:19:56.590 --> 00:19:59.820
I think at one level all of these are ways

00:19:59.820 --> 00:20:03.600
of trying to soften or appropriate

00:20:03.600 --> 00:20:06.250
the term innovation or transform it

00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:09.720
so that the element of it that has to do

00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:12.440
with novelty and invention is retained

00:20:15.170 --> 00:20:19.400
but you shift the discussion
to ask innovation for whom?

00:20:19.400 --> 00:20:24.400
And I think it's that for
whom that is now a new framing

00:20:24.605 --> 00:20:28.520
that is necessary in order
to get people to think

00:20:28.520 --> 00:20:31.293
differently about the
gains from innovation.

00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:34.973
- I agree.

00:20:34.973 --> 00:20:36.230
I mean, my fear

00:20:37.610 --> 00:20:41.210
is that soon and since we're all

00:20:41.210 --> 00:20:44.390
sort of sociopolitical
economists in this panel,

00:20:44.390 --> 00:20:47.320
that soon if we will continue
to have social innovation,

00:20:47.320 --> 00:20:51.230
inclusive innovation, innovation
will end up like culture

00:20:51.230 --> 00:20:55.090
meaning it is encompassing everything

00:20:55.090 --> 00:20:57.890
and has zero meaning by itself.

00:20:57.890 --> 00:21:00.640
And I'm very worried about that.

00:21:00.640 --> 00:21:03.710
So I would say the good thing about it,

00:21:03.710 --> 00:21:07.690
those hyphen of innovation
is that it shows

00:21:07.690 --> 00:21:10.790
that people realise how
important innovation is

00:21:10.790 --> 00:21:13.080
to their welfare and to their society.

00:21:13.080 --> 00:21:17.060
Indeed, we will all be stuck
in a room without innovation,

00:21:17.060 --> 00:21:18.230
all kinds of innovation,

00:21:18.230 --> 00:21:20.420
but people who came with the vaccines

00:21:20.420 --> 00:21:23.360
and as we in Canada knows
that people who actually

00:21:23.360 --> 00:21:27.913
know how to produce the
vaccines are equally important.

00:21:29.900 --> 00:21:34.270
But I'm worried that those
hyphenated innovation

00:21:34.270 --> 00:21:38.820
has now moving the
debate slightly offside.

00:21:38.820 --> 00:21:41.690
And we are not asking the
really important question

00:21:41.690 --> 00:21:45.003
about innovation, growth,
equity, and prosperity anymore.

00:21:49.760 --> 00:21:51.860
- There's a question from the audience

00:21:53.210 --> 00:21:55.620
that's kind of bringing
us back in some ways

00:21:55.620 --> 00:21:57.380
to where we started in talking

00:21:57.380 --> 00:22:00.200
about kind of place-based innovation.

00:22:00.200 --> 00:22:01.990
And in this case it's specifically

00:22:01.990 --> 00:22:03.883
coming from Alberta, Canada,

00:22:04.760 --> 00:22:08.300
where the kind of
innovation in the economy

00:22:08.300 --> 00:22:09.300
has really been dominated

00:22:09.300 --> 00:22:12.290
by fossil fuels in the energy sector.

00:22:12.290 --> 00:22:14.630
Although, arguably now,
there seems to be a shift

00:22:14.630 --> 00:22:17.738
to some degree to become, you
know, some version (laughs)

00:22:17.738 --> 00:22:22.040
as you pointed out Silicon
Valley of the North

00:22:22.040 --> 00:22:25.500
with the tech driven focus around AI

00:22:25.500 --> 00:22:29.200
and whether or not an approach
like that makes sense.

00:22:29.200 --> 00:22:31.290
Again, I think the important part here

00:22:31.290 --> 00:22:34.310
is given Alberta centrality around,

00:22:34.310 --> 00:22:36.103
you know, fossil fuels and energy.

00:22:38.130 --> 00:22:43.130
- So I'll start and Woody you can chip in.

00:22:43.370 --> 00:22:46.370
What is interesting for me about Canada,

00:22:46.370 --> 00:22:47.203
speaking about fossil,

00:22:47.203 --> 00:22:49.780
it's that there's actually two provinces

00:22:49.780 --> 00:22:52.350
for playing this game as we speak.

00:22:52.350 --> 00:22:54.600
One is Alberta and the
other is Newfoundland.

00:22:56.370 --> 00:22:57.720
And they have both chosen

00:22:57.720 --> 00:23:00.773
completely different
strategies in the past.

00:23:02.920 --> 00:23:06.280
And then we have another
case which I'm really fond of

00:23:06.280 --> 00:23:08.310
that I think Woody might know even more

00:23:08.310 --> 00:23:10.560
of a small country called Norway.

00:23:10.560 --> 00:23:11.650
- Yeah, I think I have.
- That also has

00:23:11.650 --> 00:23:13.860
lack of the same problem.

00:23:13.860 --> 00:23:16.590
So if you look across those cases,

00:23:16.590 --> 00:23:19.280
I think what is interesting
in the two cases

00:23:19.280 --> 00:23:24.280
that I am slightly more inclined to say

00:23:25.336 --> 00:23:29.440
have chosen smart based on what they have

00:23:29.440 --> 00:23:33.120
is actually, might be
Newfoundland and Norway.

00:23:33.120 --> 00:23:35.610
Because what they have done is realise

00:23:35.610 --> 00:23:38.480
what kind of skills they already have

00:23:39.550 --> 00:23:42.310
in around that industry.

00:23:42.310 --> 00:23:45.750
So it's not just AI, it's AI for mining.

00:23:45.750 --> 00:23:48.380
It's how to deal with ice.

00:23:48.380 --> 00:23:51.713
It's moving to what
people call green tech.

00:23:53.150 --> 00:23:55.830
So you take your skills of knowledge

00:23:55.830 --> 00:24:00.830
and you try to diversify
to related industries

00:24:01.290 --> 00:24:04.060
or thinking about leap frogging

00:24:04.060 --> 00:24:06.063
but from the knowledge we already have.

00:24:07.540 --> 00:24:11.970
The other thing which
I think is as important

00:24:11.970 --> 00:24:13.850
is let's assume for the moment

00:24:13.850 --> 00:24:16.123
that Alberta actually
becomes Silicon Valley.

00:24:17.140 --> 00:24:18.990
What happens to all those people

00:24:18.990 --> 00:24:23.780
who now works in the
industry at the moment.

00:24:23.780 --> 00:24:25.960
And the problem of trying to imitate

00:24:25.960 --> 00:24:27.280
and move to Silicon Valley,

00:24:27.280 --> 00:24:31.453
even if it's successful to
one or two of Alberta cities,

00:24:32.390 --> 00:24:36.660
is that it does not solve
the real looming problem.

00:24:36.660 --> 00:24:39.560
And that is that Alberta was the engine

00:24:39.560 --> 00:24:43.507
of an unbelievable amount
of really good jobs

00:24:45.130 --> 00:24:47.880
for upper middle class
people who are not graduates

00:24:47.880 --> 00:24:52.420
of the best engineering
schools for all of Canada.

00:24:52.420 --> 00:24:56.870
This is unfortunately
coming to an end, I fear.

00:24:56.870 --> 00:24:58.890
And it's not just a problem for Alberta

00:24:58.890 --> 00:25:01.880
but it's a problem for all of Canada

00:25:01.880 --> 00:25:04.870
is to figure out what kind of jobs

00:25:04.870 --> 00:25:07.690
or what kind of innovation
strategy we can have

00:25:07.690 --> 00:25:10.223
that actually supplies
good jobs for those people.

00:25:11.810 --> 00:25:14.160
Maybe thinking like North Carolina

00:25:16.010 --> 00:25:19.900
instead of thinking like
Silicon Valley might be the way.

00:25:19.900 --> 00:25:22.960
But Woody, what do you think
about Norway as a model?

00:25:22.960 --> 00:25:26.200
- Well, I was, as you started
talking, I was thinking Norway

00:25:26.200 --> 00:25:30.150
would be an interesting
example for Alberta

00:25:30.150 --> 00:25:34.183
where they take the endowments
in the ground, under the sea,

00:25:35.163 --> 00:25:39.340
in the skills and use those
endowments to make a transfer

00:25:39.340 --> 00:25:42.300
into new industries,
make investments in them.

00:25:42.300 --> 00:25:46.070
But I think maybe the
harder issue is not like

00:25:46.070 --> 00:25:47.410
what do we do on the ground

00:25:47.410 --> 00:25:49.433
but what goes on in people's minds.

00:25:50.450 --> 00:25:53.640
So in a place like Alberta, Texas,

00:25:53.640 --> 00:25:57.523
lots of places where
people have prosperity

00:26:00.520 --> 00:26:03.370
from old ways of doing things,

00:26:03.370 --> 00:26:05.190
change and doing something differently

00:26:05.190 --> 00:26:08.830
is often seen as a threat,
as a huge challenge,

00:26:08.830 --> 00:26:11.760
they're trying to take
away what we've done.

00:26:11.760 --> 00:26:16.060
But a different way to think
about innovation is not just

00:26:16.060 --> 00:26:17.670
that it's created by outsiders,

00:26:17.670 --> 00:26:20.470
but maybe insiders want to change the way

00:26:20.470 --> 00:26:23.610
they're doing things in
order to remain on top.

00:26:23.610 --> 00:26:26.290
There's a great Italian
novel, "Lampedusa",

00:26:26.290 --> 00:26:29.940
about if you want things to stay the same,

00:26:29.940 --> 00:26:31.183
you have to change.

00:26:32.040 --> 00:26:33.320
Is the theme from that.

00:26:33.320 --> 00:26:37.860
And we know Alberta, at the
moment, has the prosperity

00:26:37.860 --> 00:26:41.830
that could be used to see
beyond the world of fossil fuels

00:26:41.830 --> 00:26:43.780
whether they have the mindset do that

00:26:43.780 --> 00:26:47.130
is, of course, a very different question.

00:26:48.600 --> 00:26:51.690
- We have time for one or
two more quick questions

00:26:51.690 --> 00:26:53.590
and there's some great ones coming in.

00:26:55.130 --> 00:26:59.820
So what is the role of
corporate governance

00:26:59.820 --> 00:27:02.470
is really kind of at
the heart of the issues

00:27:02.470 --> 00:27:04.700
that you're talking about,
the distribution of power

00:27:04.700 --> 00:27:07.240
in the corporation, who
makes the decisions,

00:27:07.240 --> 00:27:09.143
who distributes the profit to whom.

00:27:10.110 --> 00:27:11.940
What corporate governance systems

00:27:11.940 --> 00:27:13.800
work better for innovation?

00:27:13.800 --> 00:27:17.260
Is this something that's
internal to the firm

00:27:17.260 --> 00:27:20.413
or is regulation from state required?

00:27:23.870 --> 00:27:26.520
- So Woody, do you want to be first or?

00:27:26.520 --> 00:27:28.600
- Ah, yeah, I guess
you want me to go first

00:27:28.600 --> 00:27:31.650
'cause that's one, such a
phenomenally good question

00:27:31.650 --> 00:27:34.360
and two, such an incredibly hard question.

00:27:34.360 --> 00:27:35.193
Okay.

00:27:36.310 --> 00:27:37.143
And

00:27:38.950 --> 00:27:43.030
clearly the model of how
boards ought to operate

00:27:43.030 --> 00:27:45.820
or could operate in the context

00:27:45.820 --> 00:27:50.820
of entrepreneurial innovation
has been a huge challenge.

00:27:51.170 --> 00:27:53.287
Because the people are running the boards

00:27:53.287 --> 00:27:55.090
have either been the VCs

00:27:55.090 --> 00:27:58.850
who backed the companies or the founders

00:27:58.850 --> 00:28:01.720
often with special
shares that allowed them

00:28:01.720 --> 00:28:06.720
to have enormous influence for
a continuing period of time.

00:28:07.850 --> 00:28:10.520
And thinking about novelty, and invention,

00:28:10.520 --> 00:28:13.050
and corporate governance, I
think this is as important

00:28:13.050 --> 00:28:15.570
as thinking about product innovation.

00:28:15.570 --> 00:28:19.100
So in one sense, I'm punting
to say there's not enough work

00:28:19.100 --> 00:28:22.920
being done on figuring out
different models of governance

00:28:22.920 --> 00:28:26.620
that might have a longer time horizon

00:28:26.620 --> 00:28:29.750
and also think more broadly about

00:28:29.750 --> 00:28:33.350
who the beneficiaries of companies are.

00:28:33.350 --> 00:28:35.300
There's some examples.

00:28:35.300 --> 00:28:37.980
They're often in somewhat exotic places

00:28:37.980 --> 00:28:41.370
but now there are companies
turning to be B Corporations.

00:28:41.370 --> 00:28:43.240
For example, let's say we have

00:28:43.240 --> 00:28:46.300
both financial and social goals.

00:28:46.300 --> 00:28:47.990
There are others that
have tried to bring in

00:28:47.990 --> 00:28:50.060
stay cut different stakeholders.

00:28:50.060 --> 00:28:51.700
But we need fresh thought

00:28:51.700 --> 00:28:55.300
about innovation and
corporate governance, I think.

00:28:55.300 --> 00:28:56.380
Dan.

00:28:56.380 --> 00:28:59.540
- So I'll just add that
it's not just the corporate.

00:28:59.540 --> 00:29:02.036
Just look at what happened to Boeing

00:29:02.036 --> 00:29:04.197
and then start to think why it happened.

00:29:04.197 --> 00:29:07.260
And it happened because we also change

00:29:07.260 --> 00:29:10.010
what are the legal requirement

00:29:10.010 --> 00:29:14.585
from a CEO, a CFO, to the shareholders.

00:29:14.585 --> 00:29:18.890
And the end result is Boeing,
a company which actually makes

00:29:18.890 --> 00:29:23.890
a lot of its money on defense
catering more to short-termism

00:29:24.600 --> 00:29:27.430
on Wall Street and making decision

00:29:27.430 --> 00:29:29.600
where to allocate R and D funds

00:29:29.600 --> 00:29:34.520
on changing body frames of
aeroplanes  but ended up

00:29:34.520 --> 00:29:38.123
with a completely unnecessary
disaster like for Max.

00:29:38.960 --> 00:29:41.950
Partly because it was
decision we're taking 15 years

00:29:41.950 --> 00:29:46.510
before saying we are not
allowed, legally allowed,

00:29:46.510 --> 00:29:50.850
to make those massive investment
in the long-term R and D

00:29:50.850 --> 00:29:52.770
because the CEO would lose his job

00:29:52.770 --> 00:29:56.740
and the real job that I have
now, corporate governance,

00:29:56.740 --> 00:30:01.540
is to make shareholders
happy now and always now.

00:30:01.540 --> 00:30:05.170
And that's a big issue that
I think we have to tackle

00:30:05.170 --> 00:30:07.870
but I think we're running
out of time in this session.

00:30:09.150 --> 00:30:12.610
- In fact, that is all
we have time for today.

